
  riscvsim › demo 3 · Skipping the wait
  Pass a result straight to the next instruction.

   1  # Each add needs the result just computed. Forwarding
   2  # hands it over between stages, so nothing waits.
   3  addi x5, x0, 5     # x5 = 5
   4  addi x6, x5, 3     # needs x5: forwarded, x6 = 8
   5  add  x7, x6, x5    # needs x6 and x5: x7 = 13

  cycle 1
    IF addi x5, x0, 5  ID ·  EX ·  MEM ·  WB ·
    › The first instruction, addi, is fetched from memory.

  cycle 2
    IF addi x6, x5, 3  ID addi x5, x0, 5  EX ·  MEM ·  WB ·
    › addi x5 is decoded and reads its source registers.

  cycle 3
    IF add x7, x6, x5  ID addi x6, x5, 3  EX addi x5, x0, 5  MEM ·  WB ·
    › addi x5 is computing in the ALU; everything else moves one stage on.

  cycle 4
    IF ·  ID add x7, x6, x5  EX addi x6, x5, 3  MEM addi x5, x0, 5  WB ·
    › addi x6 needs x5 from addi x5, so it is forwarded from the EX/MEM
      pipeline register and nobody waits.
      Forwarding: a result goes straight to the instruction that needs it,
      before it is written to its register.
      A pipeline register holds an instruction and its values between two
      stages.

  cycle 5
    IF ·  ID ·  EX add x7, x6, x5  MEM addi x6, x5, 3  WB addi x5, x0, 5
    ✓ x5 = 5
    › add needs x6 from addi x6 and x5 from addi x5, so both are forwarded
      from pipeline registers and nobody waits.

  cycle 6
    IF ·  ID ·  EX ·  MEM add x7, x6, x5  WB addi x6, x5, 3
    ✓ x6 = 8
    › addi finishes and writes x6 = 8.

  cycle 7
    IF ·  ID ·  EX ·  MEM ·  WB add x7, x6, x5
    ✓ x7 = 13
    ✓ add writes x7 = 13, and the program is finished.

  ✓ 7 cycles · 3 instructions · CPI 2.33 · 0 stalls · 0 mispredictions
    Ends with x5 = 5 · x6 = 8 · x7 = 13
    No stalls: forwarding kept every instruction moving.

